# Example MTEB leaderboard

Typical high-performing models include:

* BGE Large
* BGE M3
* E5 Large
* GTE Large
* NV-Embed
* Stella
* Jina Embeddings
* OpenAI text-embedding-3-large

The higher the MTEB score, the better the model generally performs across many tasks.

---

# Why should a RAG engineer care?

Because retrieval is one of the MTEB categories.

When you see:

```
BGE-large-en-v1.5
MTEB Retrieval Score: 54
```

it means:

> On average, BGE retrieves relevant documents better than a model scoring 45.

For production RAG systems, MTEB retrieval scores are often a good starting signal when choosing an embedding model.

---

# Example with MiniLM vs BGE

Suppose:

| Model                         | Dimension |        MTEB Score |
| ----------------------------- | --------: | ----------------: |
| all-MiniLM-L6-v2              |       384 |            ~55-60 |
| bge-base-en-v1.5              |       768 |               ~63 |
| bge-large-en-v1.5             |      1024 |              ~65+ |
| OpenAI text-embedding-3-large |      3072 |         ~high 60s |
| NV-Embed-v2                   |      4096 | among the highest |

Higher MTEB usually means better retrieval quality, but also:

* larger vectors
* more VRAM
* more storage
* slower indexing

So production systems balance:

```
Quality (MTEB)
vs
Cost
vs
Latency
```

---

# For your RAG learning

When you read:

```python
embeddings = HuggingFaceEmbeddings(
    model_name="sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"
)
```

that model is chosen mostly because:

* tiny
* fast
* easy to run on a laptop

Not because it is the highest-scoring MTEB model.

In real production RAG systems, it is common to use stronger models such as BGE, E5, GTE, NV-Embed, Jina, or OpenAI embeddings because they generally achieve higher MTEB retrieval performance and therefore retrieve more relevant chunks.
